RewriteEngine On

# 2026-10-29 - WHY NOTHING IN THIS FILE EVER FIRED, AND WHY IT WILL NOW (read before "fixing" anything here).
# This file never reached the server. .cpanel.yml copied the site with `cp -R *`, and a shell `*` skips
# dot-files at the top level, so /public_html never received it - every "this host ignores .htaccess"
# note below was written about a file that was not there. The host DOES honour .htaccess: the deployed
# forms/_submissions/.htaccess ("Require all denied") turns a real file there into a 403, verified
# 2026-10-29. .cpanel.yml now copies this file explicitly. A git revert does NOT remove it from the host
# (the deploy never deletes): to take it down, delete public_html/.htaccess in cPanel File Manager.
#
# OTHER SITES LIVE UNDER THIS FOLDER. sales.wooldridgeboats.com (Tyler's dealer site) and
# shop.wooldridgeboats.com have their document roots inside public_html (/sales, /shop), and Apache
# applies this file to them too - the old WordPress .htaccess did exactly that to dev. So every rule
# here is held to www and the bare domain by an HTTP_HOST condition. Keep it that way.

# Redirect index.html requests to the folder (e.g. /contact/index.html -> /contact/). THE_REQUEST means
# only what the browser asked for, never Apache's own DirectoryIndex lookup, so this cannot loop.
# Absolute target (/$1) on purpose: /index.html leaves $1 empty and must land on /.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^(www\.)?wooldridgeboats\.com$ [NC]
RewriteCond %{THE_REQUEST} ^[A-Z]{3,9}\ /.*index\.html\ HTTP/
RewriteRule ^(.*/)?index\.html$ /$1 [R=301,L]

# NOTE: an earlier version of this file also tried to hide the .html
# extension on flat pages (tools/*.html) via a redirect rule plus
# either a custom RewriteRule or Options +MultiViews to serve them. Neither
# worked on this host — investigation showed .htaccess rewrite conditions
# and Options aren't being honored here, and any request that falls through
# to an actual 404 on this host returns a broken 500 instead (a separate,
# pre-existing server/hosting misconfiguration, unrelated to this file).
# All internal links have been reverted to their real .html filenames for
# those pages, so nothing on the site currently depends on that behavior.
# Revisit only after confirming with the host that AllowOverride grants
# FileInfo/Options for this vhost.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 404 handling — added 25 Aug 2026, ahead of the dev -> production cutover.
#
# WHY: this host answers a missing path with a bare "500 Internal Server Error"
# instead of a 404. Verified by effect 25 Aug 2026 — every nonexistent path on
# dev returned 500, while the old production WordPress site returned 404 for the
# same request. A 500 tells a crawler "the server is broken, distrust the whole
# site"; a 404 tells it "this one URL is retired, keep the rest". At cutover the
# seven WooCommerce URLs still serving on production (/cart/, /checkout/,
# /account/, /login/, /category/equipment/, /feed/, /comments/feed/) all become
# missing paths at once. Stephen's ruling 25 Aug: let them 404 cleanly, no
# per-URL redirects — /404.html routes people onward instead.
#
# EXPECT THIS TO BE IGNORED. The project CLAUDE.md states as a hard rule that
# this host does not honour .htaccess rewrites, and the note above records the
# same by test. ErrorDocument needs the same AllowOverride FileInfo grant, so it
# will most likely be ignored too.
#
# It is here anyway because there is NO client-side substitute for an HTTP
# status code — a stub page cannot turn a 500 into a 404. If this is ignored the
# fix is not a code change, it is a hosting ticket, and this line plus the test
# below is the evidence to hand GoDaddy.
#
# VERIFY BY EFFECT after deploying — never assume it took:
#   curl.exe -s -o NUL -w "%{http_code}`n" https://dev.wooldridgeboats.com/zzz-nope
# 404 = working, and /404.html is being served. 500 = still ignored.
# (2026-10-29: held to www and the bare domain - /404.html does not exist in the sales or shop folders.)
<If "%{HTTP_HOST} =~ /^(www\.)?wooldridgeboats\.com$/i">
ErrorDocument 404 /404.html
</If>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The old /store/ mockup -> the real Shopify store. Stephen's ruling 25 Aug 2026.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE. A RewriteRule was written at this spot first and
# then removed, for the reason above: this host does not honour rewrites.
#
# The redirect is a STUB PAGE instead — store/index.html follows the models/xp
# pattern (canonical + noindex + meta refresh + location.replace + a visible
# link). Read that file before changing anything here.
#
# Background: store/index.html was a DESIGN MOCKUP carrying hardcoded prices
# ($900, $950, $999, $949, $799, $599) and dead buttons — never a source of
# truth for price or stock. Nothing on the site linked to it, but it answered a
# public 200 with invented figures. The stub replaces that content; the mockup
# is recoverable from git history if it is ever wanted.

# BEGIN REDIRECTS - generated by _build/redirects/gen_redirects.pl from _build/redirects/old_urls.tsv. Do not hand-edit:
# change a row in old_urls.tsv and re-run the script. Old WordPress/WooCommerce addresses -> their new homes.
# Guard: nothing below fires for another host (sales./shop. live in folders under this one) or for a
# path that exists on disk (a real page or folder always wins over its old address).
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} !^(www\.)?wooldridgeboats\.com$ [NC,OR]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -f [OR]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} -d
RewriteRule ^ - [L]
